From c67d239edfdb6248eb586830f43edc4c0d4d682d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nikola Cvetkovic <ncvetkovic@tenstorrent.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 13:06:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add output blocking with tile reuse to high_power_matmul

The workload reads A and B from DRAM for every output tile with no reuse: for
each output tile the reader issues 2*Kt tile reads and the writer one write. At
Mt=32, Nt=64, Kt=64 that is 85.9 GB read per interval against 1.374e12 FLOPs, an
arithmetic intensity of 15.9 FLOP/byte -- about an order of magnitude below what
a bfloat16 matmul needs to be compute-bound. The workload therefore measures data
movement, not arithmetic, and its power and efficiency numbers are set by the
fabric rather than by the FPUs.

Adds HIGH_POWER_BLOCK_M / HIGH_POWER_BLOCK_N. Each core now accumulates a
BLOCK_M x BLOCK_N patch of output tiles in the destination registers at once, so
one column slice of A and one row slice of B feed BLOCK_M*BLOCK_N multiplies
instead of one. Tile reads per multiply fall from 2 to
(BLOCK_M + BLOCK_N) / (BLOCK_M * BLOCK_N): at 2x4 that is 0.75, a 2.67x traffic
reduction, lifting intensity to 42 FLOP/byte.

Work is split over output blocks rather than output tiles, so every grid in the
sweep still works including the ones whose core count does not divide the tile
counts -- unlike matmul_multicore_reuse, which asserts Mt % per_core_M == 0 and
so would restrict the sweep to power-of-two grids. The block size is passed as a
JIT define, so switching it recompiles only the kernels. 1x1 is the default and
reproduces the previous behaviour exactly.

Measured on p100a at 1024 2048 2048 160, POWER_CASE=0, block 2x4 against 1x1:
throughput 3.47 -> 9.05 TFLOPS at 11x10 (2.6x, matching the 2.67x traffic cut
almost exactly, which is what confirms the workload was bandwidth-bound), and
energy per FLOP 21.1 -> 7.7 pJ. Board power is unchanged at ~74 W, so the entire
energy gain comes from finishing sooner rather than from drawing less.

Also prints an output checksum. The workload never verifies its results, so with
a new tile-indexing scheme that was the only way to catch a mistake. Inputs are
seeded deterministically and the accumulation order over the shared dimension is
identical for every block size, so the checksum must not vary with block size --
and it does not: 1x1, 2x2, 2x4 and 1x8 all produce sum=-4.369405e+02
absmax=8.562500e+00 on every grid. The print sits after the timing is taken so it
cannot inflate the measured window.
---
